fs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 And since I can make my own happiness through vices natural to me, which I\uc1 \u8217 ?ve acquired without working, which I maintain without effort, which are compatible with the customs of my country, which suit the taste of those who protect me and are closer to their small particular needs than virtues which would embarrass them, by criticizing them morning and night, it would be really odd if I were to go on tormenting myself like some soul in hell in order to cut myself up and make myself something other than I am, to give myself a character foreign to my own, with very worthy qualities\uc1 \u8212 ?I\uc1 \u8217 ?ll concede that, to avoid an argument\uc1 \u8212 ?but which would cost me a great deal to acquire and to practise and would lead me to nothing at all, perhaps worse than nothing, because all the time I\uc1 \u8217 ?d have to satirize the rich people among whom beggars like me have to seek out a living. People praise virtue, but they hate it. They run away from it, because it makes them freezing cold, and in this world one has to have warm feet. Besides, it would inevitably make me moody. Why else do we so often see devout people so hard, so angry, so unsociable? It\uc1 \u8217 ?s because they\uc1 \u8217 ?ve imposed on themselves a task which isn\uc1 \u8217 ?t natural to them. They suffer, and when one suffers, one makes others suffer. That\uc1 \u8217 ?s not what I want, nor do my patrons. I have to be happy, flexible, pleasant, funny, amusing. Virtue makes itself respected, and respect is uncomfortable. Virtue makes itself admired, and admiration is not amusing. My business is with people who are bored, and I have to make them laugh. So I have to be ridiculous and funny. And if nature had not made me that way, the simplest thing would be to appear like that. Fortunately, I don\uc1 \u8217 ?t need to be a hypocrite. There are already so many of them of every stripe, without counting those who are hypocritical even with themselves. Take that Chevalier de la Morli\uc1 \u232 ?re, who turns up his hat above his ears, holds his head in the air, looks at you disdainfully as you go by, has a long sword banging against his thigh and an instant insult ready for anyone who doesn\uc1 \u8217 ?t carry one, and seems to be issuing a challenge to everyone coming along. What\uc1 \u8217 ?s he doing? Everything he can to persuade himself that he\uc1 \u8217 ?s a stout-hearted man. But he\uc1 \u8217 ?s a coward. Just tweak the end of his nose\uc1 \u8212 ?he\uc1 \u8217 ?ll take it quietly. If you want to make him lower his voice, raise you own. Show him your cane, or give him a kick in the ass. He\uc1 \u8217 ?ll be completely astonished to find out he\uc1 \u8217 ?s a coward and will ask you how you found out, who told you. The moment before he was ignorant of the fact, for his long and habitual aping of bravery had convinced him. He\uc1 \u8217 ?d gone through the pretence so many times he believed that\uc1 \u8217 ?s what he was. And that woman who mortifies herself, who visits prisons, helps at all the charitable meetings, walks along with her eyes lowered, and would never dare to look a man in the face, always on guard against being seduced by her senses\uc1 \u8212 ?does all that keep her heart from burning, sighs escaping from her, her temperament catching fire, her desires obsessing her, and her imagination going over and over night and day scenes from the }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Portier des Chartreux }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 or the }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \