ngfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 HIM}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \line }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 I\uc1 \u8217 ?ve already told you we have no effect. We insult the entire world and don\uc1 \u8217 ?t hurt anyone. Sometimes our company includes the lumbering Abb\uc1 \u233 ? d\uc1 \u8217 ?Olivet, the fat Abb\uc1 \u233 ? Le Blanc, and the hypocrite Batteux. The fat abb\uc1 \u233 ? is malicious only before he\uc1 \u8217 ?s eaten. Once he\uc1 \u8217 ?s had his coffee, he throws himself into an armchair, rests his feet against the shelf by the chimney, and snoozes like an old parrot on its perch. If the noise gets violent, he yawns, stretches his arms, rubs his eyes, and says, \uc1 \u8220 ?All right, what\uc1 \u8217 ?s up? What is it?\uc1 \u8221 ? \uc1 \u8220 ?We\uc1 \u8217 ?re trying to find out if Piron has more wit than Voltaire.\uc1 \u8221 ? \uc1 \u8220 ?Let\uc1 \u8217 ?s get this straight\uc1 \u8212 ?are you talking about wit? It\uc1 \u8217 ?s not a question of taste, for your Piron has no notion of taste.\uc1 \u8221 ? \uc1 \u8220 ?No idea at all?\uc1 \u8221 ? \uc1 \u8220 ?No.\uc1 \u8221 ? And then we set out on a discussion of taste. Then our patron signals with his hand that we should listen to him, because he\uc1 \u8217 ?s keener on taste than on anything, \uc1 \u8220 ?Taste,\uc1 \u8221 ? he says, \uc1 \u8220 ?taste is something . . .\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u8212 ?my goodness I\uc1 \u8217 ?ve no idea what he said it was, and neither does he. Sometimes our friend Robb\uc1 \u233 ? is with us. He amuses us with cynical stories, the miracles of the Convulsionists, which he witnessed himself, and also with a few cantos from his poem on a subject which he knows really well.}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \cs16 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \super1 \chftn {\footnote \pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sa60 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \cs16 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \super1 \chftn }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Abb\uc1 \u233 ? d\uc1 \u8217 ?Olivet (1682-1768): a French cleric and writer; Abb\uc1 \u233 ? Le Blanc (1707-1781): a French art critic with considerable political influence; Charles Batteux (1713-1780): a French philosopher and art critic; Alexis Piron (1689-1773): a French dramatist; Robb\uc1 \u233 ? de Beauveset (1725-1794): a minor poet. Convulsionists: fanatics who went into convulsions as part of their religious experience.}}}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  I hate his verses, but I like to hear him recite. He looks like a maniac. All those around him cry out, \uc1 \u8220 ?Now that\uc1 \u8217 ?s what we call a poet.\uc1 \u8221 ? Just between us, this poetry is nothing but a clamour of all sorts of confused noises, the barbarous chant of people living in the Tower of Babel. Sometimes we also get a visit from a certain simpleton with a dull, stupid expression but a mind like a demon\uc1 \u8212 ?he\uc1 \u8217 ?s smarter than an old monkey. He\uc1 \u8217 ?s one of those figures who invite jokes and tricks, someone God made to straighten out people who judge on the basis of appearances\uc1 \u8212 ?who should have learned at their own mirrors that it is just as easy to be a witty man and look like a fool as it is to hide a fool under an intelligent-looking appearance. It\uc1 \u8217 ?s a really common form of cowardice to sacrifice a good man for the amusement of others, and people never fail to go after this man. He\uc1 \u8217 ?s a trap we set for the new arrivals, and I\uc1 \u8217 ?ve hardly seen a single one of them fail to get caught.}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \fi-480 \cufi-200 \li480 \ri0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin480 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sl276 \slmult1 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 I was sometimes surprised by the justness of this fool\uc1 \u8217 ?s observations on men and their characters. I told him as much. \uc1 \u8220 ?Well,\uc1 \u8221 ? he replied, \uc1 \u8220 ?it\uc1 \u8217 ?s a matter of getting some benefits out of bad company, just like out of being a libertine. You get compensation for the loss of innocence b