rhaps you\uc1 \u8217 ?re my fool. A man who wanted to be wise would not have a fool. That\uc1 \u8217 ?s why anyone who has a fool is not wise. If he\uc1 \u8217 ?s not wise, he\uc1 \u8217 ?s a fool and perhaps, if he\uc1 \u8217 ?s a king, his Fool\uc1 \u8217 ?s fool. Beyond this, you should remember that in a subject as varied as morals, there\uc1 \u8217 ?s nothing absolutely, essentially, and universally true or false, unless it\uc1 \u8217 ?s the fact that one has to be what one\uc1 \u8217 ?s self-interest wants one to be, good or bad, wise or foolish, decent or ridiculous, honest or vicious. If by chance virtue had led the way to a fortune, either I\uc1 \u8217 ?d have been virtuous, or I\uc1 \u8217 ?d have pretended to be virtuous, just like anyone else. People wanted me to be ridiculous, and that\uc1 \u8217 ?s what I\uc1 \u8217 ?ve made myself. As for viciousness, nature alone took care of that. When I say vicious, it\uc1 \u8217 ?s in order to speak your language, for if we were to come to an understanding of each other, it could turn out that you call vice what I call virtue and virtue what I call vice. We also had among us authors from the Op\uc1 \u233 ?ra Comique, their actors and actresses, and more often their managers Corby, Moette . . . all resourceful people of superior merit. And I was forgetting the great literary critics\uc1 \u8212 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 L}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u8217 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Avant-Coureur}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lan