o\uc1 \u8217 ?d ever heard a beggar asking for a hand out in the street, a man carried away by anger, a jealous and furious woman, a despairing lover, a flatterer, yes, a flatterer, softening his voice, drawing out his syllables in a voice like honey\uc1 \u8212 ?in short, anyone who\uc1 \u8217 ?d ever heard passion of some sort or other, provided that its energy made it worthy of serving as a model for a composer, should have recognized two things: first, that the syllables, long or short, have no fixed length, nor even a set relationship between their lengths and, second, that passion uses prosody almost as it likes\uc1 \u8212 ?it can work across the greatest intervals. A man who cries out in the depths of his grief, \uc1 \u8216 ?Ah, what an unhappy creature I am,\uc1 \u8217 ? lifts the opening syllable of exclamation to the highest and shrillest note and brings the others down to the most solemn and lowest notes, going through the octave or an even greater interval, giving each sound the quantity which suits the turn of the melody, without offending the ear and without either the long or the short syllables maintaining the length or brevity of normal speech. How far we\uc1 \u8217 ?ve come since the time when we used to point to the parenthetical comments in }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Armide: Le vainquer de Renaud }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 (}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 si quelqu'un le peut \uc1 \u234 ?tre}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang10